WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.980
Hey, welcome to Fascinating Frontiers Episode 100.

00:00:03.460 --> 00:00:05.600
Today is June 13th, 2026.

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:08.340
Let's look at what's new across the frontiers

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:09.520
of space and science.

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:12.540
That's Episode 100, a big milestone.

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:13.980
Thank you for being here.

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:17.660
NASA's X-59 jet just broke the sound barrier

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:19.840
for the first time while aiming to replace

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:22.620
thunderous sonic booms with a quiet thump.

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:25.900
The experimental aircraft reached Mach 1

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:28.920
while engineers measured the resulting pressure signature

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:29.760
on the ground.

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:34.060
NASA designed the long slender shape to reshape the shock waves

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:36.860
into a softer thump rather than a loud boom.

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:39.940
This milestone advances the agency's goal

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:42.180
of enabling supersonic flight over land

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:43.820
without disturbing communities.

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:47.280
Follow-on flights will refine the acoustic data

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:49.620
before any potential regulatory changes.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:53.460
The long slender fuselage was chosen specifically

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:56.640
to control how pressure waves propagate to the surface.

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:01.560
A record-breaking quasar that existed 12.9 billion years ago

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:04.480
already hosted a black hole of enormous mass.

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:07.320
Its spectrum shows rapid accretion

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:10.040
despite the limited time available after the big bang.

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:12.980
Spectre already show the surrounding gas

00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:15.180
as being pushed outward at high speeds.

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:18.620
The record-breaking quasar offers a rare window

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:20.860
into how supermassive black holes assembled

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:23.480
when the universe was less than a billion years old.

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:26.540
Its extreme luminosity and rapid growth

00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:28.840
imply seed masses or accretion rates

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:30.800
that current models struggle to produce.

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:34.040
Follow-up observations with larger telescopes

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:37.040
will search for similar objects at even greater distances.

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:40.760
Next, larger surveys will hunt for more examples

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:44.080
at comparable distances to test whether this object

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:45.700
was typical or an outlier.

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:48.980
Now shifting to a modern engineering effort

00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:50.680
reshaping flight over Earth.

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:54.880
The experimental X-59 aircraft reached Mach-1

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:57.700
while engineers measured the resulting pressure signature

00:01:57.700 --> 00:01:58.360
on the ground.

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:02.680
NASA designed the long slender shape to reshape the shock waves

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:05.360
into a softer thump rather than a loud boom.

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:08.360
This milestone advances the agency's goal

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:10.600
of enabling supersonic flight over land

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:12.200
without disturbing communities.

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:15.380
Follow-on flights will refine the acoustic data

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:17.920
before any potential regulatory changes.

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:21.280
Ground sensors captured detailed pressure data

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:23.140
during the initial supersonic run.

00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:26.600
While engineers quiet the skies, another crew prepares

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:29.740
for a far noisier descent near the lunar South Pole.

00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:33.220
The four person Artemis III crew discusses

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:35.440
how test-flying experience prepares them

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:38.740
for the complexities of landing near the lunar South Pole.

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:42.340
The episode of Houston we have a podcast captures

00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:44.600
their reactions to receiving the assignment

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:47.460
through an unexpected HR meeting format.

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:50.760
Listeners hear directly about the training timeline

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:54.440
leading to the first crewed lunar landing since 1972.

00:02:55.340 --> 00:02:58.000
The discussion highlights coordination between Orion,

00:02:58.580 --> 00:03:00.860
the human landing system, and Gateway elements

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:01.900
still in development.

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:05.540
The crew described how their prior test flight backgrounds

00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:08.540
directly informed decisions about descent trajectories

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:10.620
and hazard avoidance near the South Pole.

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:13.380
As Artemis crews trained for the moon,

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:16.280
a routine cargo mission is already heading home

00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:17.440
from low Earth orbit.

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:20.580
The uncrewed space X Dragon will depart

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:22.120
the International Space Station,

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:24.920
carrying completed experiments and station hardware.

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:28.260
NASA will provide live coverage of the undocking

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:29.620
and splash-down sequence.

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:33.060
The 34th Space X resupply mission

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:35.040
marks another routine cargo return

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:37.840
in the ongoing commercial resupply cadence.

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:40.520
Watch Windows open next Tuesday

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:42.740
with real-time updates from mission control.

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:45.400
The spacecraft is expected to return

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:47.780
a variety of scientific samples collected

00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:49.780
during its time birthed at the station.

00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:52.180
While Dragon heads for the Pacific,

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:54.080
we look back to the first craft

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:56.120
that left the solar system entirely.

00:03:56.740 --> 00:04:00.660
On June 13, 1983, the Pioneer 10 spacecraft

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:02.780
became the first to leave the solar system

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:04.040
on an escaped trajectory.

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:07.180
It had already achieved the first flybyes

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:08.880
of Jupiter and the asteroid belt,

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:11.180
plus the first crossing beyond Mars.

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:14.700
Radio contact continued until 2003,

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:17.040
after which the probe continued outward

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:19.160
at roughly 12 kilometers per second.

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:22.360
Its plaque remains the first physical message

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:24.720
sent toward potential interstellar recipients.

00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:27.400
The spacecraft's trajectory took it

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:29.100
through previously unexplored regions

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:31.880
of the outer solar system before across the helial pause.

00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:34.140
From the edge of the solar system,

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:35.800
we turned to a swarm concept

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:37.920
that could tag many asteroids at once.

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:41.400
The white paper outlines a fleet of small satellites

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:44.460
that would rendezvous with multiple near-earth objects

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:46.160
and attach tracking beacons.

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:50.140
Remote sensing alone cannot determine internal composition,

00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:52.660
so the probes would provide direct surface

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:54.160
and subsurface measurements.

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:57.860
The concept was submitted to the UK Space Agency's

00:04:57.860 --> 00:05:00.360
2035 Space Frontiers Program.

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:04.000
Multiple targets could be visited with far lower mass

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:05.720
than a single large spacecraft.

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:08.580
The Ramora approach would allow repeated

00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:12.180
close contact measurements across a range of asteroid types.

00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:15.020
From tagging asteroids, we moved to a rover

00:05:15.020 --> 00:05:17.080
that literally changed shape on the moon.

00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:19.940
The autonomous sphere rolled across the surface

00:05:19.940 --> 00:05:22.240
before unfolding into a wheeled robot

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:23.480
for closer inspection.

00:05:24.020 --> 00:05:26.700
It operated for a short period in 2024,

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:29.220
demonstrating shape-shifting mobility

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:30.580
without human intervention.

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:32.940
The design addressed the challenge

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:34.620
of traversing both flat planes

00:05:34.620 --> 00:05:37.060
and small craters with one platform.

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:40.340
Engineers are now evaluating similar concepts

00:05:40.340 --> 00:05:42.480
for future lunar and asteroid missions.

00:05:43.580 --> 00:05:45.920
The Japanese rover's transformation sequence

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:48.200
was completed autonomously after touchdown

00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:49.320
on the lunar surface.

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:53.140
After lunar mobility, Japan's flagship rocket

00:05:53.140 --> 00:05:55.480
has also returned to reliable service.

00:05:56.160 --> 00:05:58.360
Japan's new flagship vehicle lifted off

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:01.060
from Tanigashima carrying six satellites

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:03.020
after last year's payload loss.

00:06:03.500 --> 00:06:05.920
The flight demonstrated reliable performance

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:08.000
of the main engines and upper stage.

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:11.360
Recovery of the launch cadence supports to pants plans

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:13.700
for both government and commercial payloads.

00:06:14.360 --> 00:06:16.200
Additional missions are scheduled to restore

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:18.520
full operational confidence in the system.

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:23.160
The successful H3 flight marks a return to operational status

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:25.220
following the previous missions anomaly.

